The '''2022 Redbridge London Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022. All 63 members of [[Redbridge London Borough Council]] were elected. The elections took place alongside [[2022 London local elections|local elections in the other London boroughs]] and [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|elections to local authorities across the United Kingdom]].

In the [[2018 Redbridge London Borough Council election|previous election in 2018]], the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] maintained its control of the council, winning 51 out of the 63 seats with the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] forming the council opposition with the remaining twelve seats.

== Background ==

=== History ===
[[File:Redbridge London UK local election 2018 map.svg|thumb|Result of the 2018 borough election]]
The thirty-two [[London boroughs]] were established in 1965 by the [[London Government Act 1963]]. They are the principal authorities in Greater London and have responsibilities including education, housing, planning, highways, social services, libraries, recreation, waste, environmental health and revenue collection. Some of the powers are shared with the [[Greater London Authority]], which also manages passenger transport, police and fire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The essential guide to London local government {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/essential-guide-london-local-government|access-date=2021-10-10|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref>

Since its formation, Redbridge has generally been under [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] control or [[no overall control]]. The [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] won its first majority on the council in the [[2014 Redbridge London Borough Council election|2014 election]], winning 35 seats, while the Conservatives won 25, and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] won three. In the [[2018 Redbridge London Borough Council election|most recent election in 2018]], Labour extended its majority to win 51 seats, with 58.4% of the vote across the borough, while the Conservatives won the remaining 12 seats on 35.3% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Holder|first=Josh|title=Local council elections 2018 – results in full|url=http://www.theguardian.com/politics/ng-interactive/2018/may/04/local-council-election-results-2018-in-full|access-date=2021-10-10|website=The Guardian|language=en}}</ref>

=== Council term ===

In August 2020, Stuart Bellwood, a Labour councillor for Seven Kings, died. He had served as a councillor since 2002.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Chacko|first=Roy|date=2020-08-25|title=Tributes paid to 'maverick' councillor and 'champion of Seven Kings' Stuart Bellwood|url=https://www.ilfordrecorder.co.uk/news/colleagues-honour-maverick-labour-councillor-3267208|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Ilford Recorder|language=en-UK}}</ref> A Labour councillor for Loxford ward, Chaudhary Mohammed Iqbal, resigned in October 2020 citing health reasons.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Chacko|first=Roy|date=2020-10-09|title=Redbridge councillor awaiting electoral fraud trial resigns amidst cabinet shake-up|url=https://www.ilfordrecorder.co.uk/news/local-council/loxford-cllr-resigns-and-cabinet-member-sacked-6536212|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Ilford Recorder|language=en-UK}}</ref> He was later found guilty of electoral fraud for giving a false address.<ref>{{Cite web|last=reporter|first=Victoria Munro, Local democracy|date=2021-01-04|title=Former councillor jailed for electoral fraud said he wanted to help people|url=https://www.ilfordrecorder.co.uk/news/crime/ex-councillor-admits-electoral-fraud-6882588|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Ilford Recorder|language=en-UK}}</ref> Due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], by-elections for both seats were delayed until 6 May 2021 alongside the [[2021 London mayoral election]] and [[2021 London Assembly election|London Assembly election]]. Labour held both seats, with Sadhia Warraich winning Loxford and [[Pushpita Gupta]] winning Seven Kings.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Boyle|first=Caislin|date=2021-05-10|title=New councillors for Loxford and Seven Kings react to by-election wins|url=https://www.ilfordrecorder.co.uk/news/two-labour-councillors-join-redbridge-ranks-7959110|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Ilford Recorder|language=en-UK}}</ref>

In April 2021, Robin Turbefield, a Conservative councillor for Bridge ward, defected to [[Reform UK]] due to his opposition to lockdown measures in response to COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Service|first=Victoria Munro, Local Democracy Reporting|date=2021-04-23|title=Tory councillor defects to Reform UK|url=https://www.ilfordrecorder.co.uk/news/conservative-councillor-defects-to-reform-uk-7920094|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Ilford Recorder|language=en-UK}}</ref> In November 2021, Khaled Noor, a councillor for Barkingside, had the Labour whip removed due to his conduct.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Redbridge Labour councillor has whip removed after 'poor conduct'|url=https://www.guardian-series.co.uk/news/19709654.redbridge-labour-councillor-whip-removed-poor-conduct/|access-date=2021-11-13|website=East London and West Essex Guardian Series|date=11 November 2021 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Harpin|first=Lee|title=Councillor has whip removed after alleged 'offensive, misogynistic, antisemitic' rant|url=https://jewishnews.timesofisrael.com/cllr-has-whip-removed-after-alleged-offensive-misogynistic-antisemitic-rant/|access-date=2021-11-13|website=jewishnews.timesofisrael.com|date=10 November 2021 |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Electoral process ==
Redbridge, like other London borough councils, elects all of its councillors at once every four years. The previous election took place in 2018. The election took place by multi-member [[first-past-the-post voting]], with each ward being represented by two or three councillors. Electors had as many votes as there are councillors to be elected in their ward, with the top two or three being elected.

All [[Elections in the United Kingdom#Registration procedure|registered electors]] ([[British citizen|British]], [[Irish citizen|Irish]], [[Commonwealth citizen|Commonwealth]] and [[European Union]] citizens) living in London aged 18 or over were entitled to vote in the election. People who lived at two addresses in different councils, such as university students with different term-time and holiday addresses, were entitled to be registered for and vote in elections in both local authorities.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=How the elections work {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/london-elections/how-elections-work|access-date=2021-09-14|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref> Voting in-person at polling stations took place from 7:00 to 22:00 on election day, and voters were able to apply for postal votes or proxy votes in advance of the election.<ref name=":1" />

